<BlogPost title={title} description={description} publishDate={publishDate} category={category} relatedTools={relatedTools} locale="en">

<div class="lead">
  <p>Meta titles and descriptions are your first impression in search results. Get them right, and you'll see more clicks. Get them wrong, and you're invisible even when you rank.</p>
  <p>This guide shows you exactly how to write meta tags that rank higher and get more clicks in 2026.</p>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>What Are Meta Titles and Descriptions?</h2>
  <p>Meta titles and descriptions are HTML tags that summarize your page content for search engines:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meta title (title tag):</strong> The blue clickable headline in search results</li>
    <li><strong>Meta description:</strong> The gray text preview below the title</li>
  </ul>
  <p>They don't appear on your actual webpage - only in search results, browser tabs, and social media shares.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Why Meta Tags Matter for SEO</h2>

  <h3>Direct Ranking Factor</h3>
  <p>Google uses your meta title as a major ranking signal. Include your target keyword naturally in the title, and you're more likely to rank for that term.</p>

  <h3>Click-Through Rate Booster</h3>
  <p>Meta descriptions don't directly affect rankings, but they massively influence clicks. A compelling description can double your click-through rate, sending more traffic your way.</p>

  <h3>Social Media Previews</h3>
  <p>When someone shares your link on Facebook, LinkedIn, or Twitter, your meta tags determine what they see. Good tags = more clicks from social media.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How to Write Perfect Meta Titles</h2>

  <h3>1. Keep It Under 60 Characters</h3>
  <p>Google typically displays the first 50-60 characters. Longer titles get truncated with "..."</p>
  <p><strong>Good:</strong> "Free PDF Compressor | Reduce File Size by 90%"</p>
  <p><strong>Too long:</strong> "Free Online PDF Compressor Tool to Reduce Your PDF File Sizes Without Losing Quality"</p>

  <h3>2. Put Keywords First</h3>
  <p>Front-load your most important keyword. Google gives more weight to words at the beginning.</p>
  <p><strong>Good:</strong> "PDF Compressor Free | No Sign-Up Required"</p>
  <p><strong>Weak:</strong> "Free, Easy, Fast PDF File Size Reducer and Compressor"</p>

  <h3>3. Include Your Brand (Sometimes)</h3>
  <p>For homepage and key landing pages, add your brand name at the end:</p>
  <p>"PDF Tools Free Online | yoimagine"</p>
  <p>For blog posts and tool pages, skip the brand to save space for keywords.</p>

  <h3>4. Make It Compelling</h3>
  <p>Your title competes with 9 other results on page 1. Stand out with:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Numbers: "7 Ways to Compress PDF Files"</li>
    <li>Year: "Best PDF Tools 2026"</li>
    <li>Benefit: "Reduce PDF Size by 90%"</li>
    <li>Free/Easy: "Free PDF Merger - No Sign-Up"</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How to Write Meta Descriptions That Get Clicks</h2>

  <h3>1. Aim for 150-160 Characters</h3>
  <p>Google shows about 120-160 characters on desktop, slightly less on mobile. Use every character wisely.</p>

  <h3>2. Include Your Primary Keyword</h3>
  <p>Google bolds matching keywords in search results, making your result more eye-catching.</p>

  <h3>3. Write Like an Ad</h3>
  <p>You're competing for attention. Use action words:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Learn how to..."</li>
    <li>"Discover the..."</li>
    <li>"Get instant..."</li>
    <li>"Try our free..."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Add a Call-to-Action</h3>
  <p>Tell readers what to do:</p>
  <p>"Try it free now" / "Download instantly" / "No sign-up required"</p>

  <h3>5. Mention Key Benefits</h3>
  <p>Why should someone click your result?</p>
  <p><strong>Good:</strong> "Compress PDF files up to 90% smaller. Free, no watermarks, works in your browser. No software download required."</p>
  <p><strong>Weak:</strong> "This is a PDF compression tool that makes your files smaller."</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Common Meta Tag Mistakes to Avoid</h2>

  <h3>Mistake 1: Keyword Stuffing</h3>
  <p><strong>Bad:</strong> "PDF Compressor, Compress PDF, PDF Compression, Reduce PDF Size, PDF Tool"</p>
  <p>This looks spammy and Google may rewrite it. Use keywords naturally.</p>

  <h3>Mistake 2: Duplicate Meta Tags</h3>
  <p>Every page needs unique meta tags. Never copy-paste the same title/description across multiple pages.</p>

  <h3>Mistake 3: Missing Keywords</h3>
  <p>If your target keyword isn't in your meta title, you're handicapping your SEO. Always include it naturally.</p>

  <h3>Mistake 4: Vague Descriptions</h3>
  <p><strong>Bad:</strong> "Welcome to our website. We offer great services."</p>
  <p><strong>Good:</strong> "Free online PDF tools - compress, merge, split, and convert PDFs instantly. No sign-up required."</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How to Test Your Meta Tags</h2>
  <p>Before publishing, preview how your tags will look:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Use our <a href="/en/tools/serp-preview/">SERP Preview tool</a> to see exactly how your result appears in Google</li>
    <li>Check character counts - titles over 60 chars and descriptions over 160 chars get cut off</li>
    <li>Verify keywords are bolded (simulate a search for your target keyword)</li>
    <li>Read it out loud - does it sound natural and compelling?</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section>
